    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle asserts that certain pairs of physical properties, like position and momentum, cannot both be measured with arbitrary precision simultaneously. Mathematically, this is expressed as $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\geq \\hbar/2$, where $\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta p$ represent the standard deviations in the measurements, and $\\hbar$ is the reduced Planck constant. This limitation is not due to flaws in experimental equipment or observer interference but is a fundamental property of quantum wavefunctions; a particle exists as a wave packet, and sharply defining its position collapses its momentum distribution. Therefore, a precise knowledge of position implies a delocalized momentum state, creating an intrinsic uncertainty that scales with the physical nature of the system itself.",
      "source": "target",
